```html
<form id="signup" novalidate>
  <label for="zip">ZIP code (5 digits)</label>
  <input id="zip" name="zip" type="text"
         pattern="\d{5}"
         data-error="Please enter exactly 5 digits."
         required>
  <span class="error" aria-live="polite"></span>
  <button type="submit">Submit</button>
</form>

<script>
  const form = document.querySelector('#signup');
  const zip = form.elements.zip;
  const errorBox = form.querySelector('.error');

  function showError() {
    if (zip.validity.valid) {
      errorBox.textContent = '';
      zip.removeAttribute('aria-invalid');
    } else {
      errorBox.textContent = zip.dataset.error;
      zip.setAttribute('aria-invalid', 'true');
    }
  }

  zip.addEventListener('input', showError);
  form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
    showError();
    if (!zip.validity.valid) e.preventDefault();
  });
</script>
```

**Why this works:** The `novalidate` attribute disables the browser's automatic bubble and submit blocking, which hands you full control over *when* and *how* errors are presented while the constraint model (`pattern`, `required`) still powers `validity`. On every `input` event we read `validity.valid` and render our own message into an `aria-live="polite"` region, so screen readers announce it without interrupting. `data-error` keeps the message next to the input in markup. We re-check on submit and call `preventDefault()` so invalid data can never reach the server — HTML validation is a UX layer, but server-side validation remains mandatory.

```html
<a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to main content</a>

<header>
  <nav>... 20 menu links ...</nav>
</header>

<main id="main-content" tabindex="-1">
  <h1>Page title</h1>
</main>

<style>
  .skip-link {
    position: absolute;
    top: -100%;
    left: 1rem;
    background: #111;
    color: #fff;
    padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem;
    z-index: 1000;
  }
  .skip-link:focus {
    top: 0;
  }
</style>
```

**Why this works:** The link is the first focusable element in the DOM, so the very first Tab press on the page reveals it, and activating it moves focus (and scrolling) to `<main>`. It is visually hidden with `top: -100%` instead of `display: none` because `display: none` would remove it from the tab order entirely; the `:focus` rule slides it back on-screen only for keyboard users. `tabindex="-1"` on `<main>` is the subtle but essential part — some browsers will not move focus to a section element targeted by a fragment link without it, which would leave the next Tab press back at the top of the menu, defeating the whole purpose.

```html
<a href="/reports">View reports</a>
<button type="button">Save draft</button>

<style>
  :root {
    --focus-ring: 0 0 0 3px #fff, 0 0 0 6px #2563eb;
  }

  :focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid transparent;
    box-shadow: var(--focus-ring);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  :focus:not(:focus-visible) {
    outline: none;
  }
</style>
```

**Why this works:** `:focus-visible` matches only when the browser determines focus should be *visually* indicated — keyboard navigation and programmatic focus for assistive tech, but not mouse clicks on most controls. That removes the classic annoyance of a persistent ring after clicking while keeping the ring exactly where keyboard users need it. The double-layer `box-shadow` (white inner, blue outer) guarantees contrast against any background; a single-color outline can vanish on same-colored surfaces. You never remove the focus indicator without providing a replacement — `outline: none` with no alternative is an accessibility failure. The `:focus:not(:focus-visible)` reset neutralizes any UA outline for pointer interactions only.

```html
<button id="open-btn">Open settings</button>

<dialog id="settings-dialog">
  <h2>Settings</h2>
  <label>
    <input type="checkbox" name="notify"> Email notifications
  </label>
  <form method="dialog">
    <button value="close">Close</button>
  </form>
</dialog>

<script>
  const dialog = document.getElementById('settings-dialog');
  document.getElementById('open-btn').addEventListener('click', () => dialog.showModal());

  dialog.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    if (e.target === dialog) dialog.close(); // backdrop click
  });
</script>

<style>
  dialog::backdrop {
    background: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.5);
    backdrop-filter: blur(2px);
  }
</style>
```

**Why this works:** `showModal()` opens the dialog in the top layer — above every other element regardless of z-index — makes the rest of the page inert (unfocusable, unclickable), traps Tab focus inside the dialog, and wires up the Escape key for free. These are the three hardest parts of DIY modals, solved natively. The backdrop-click trick works because the `::backdrop` is part of the dialog element's click target, so `e.target === dialog` is only true when the padding-less backdrop itself was hit. `method="dialog"` closes the form's dialog without submitting or reloading, and the return value is available via `dialog.returnValue`.

```html
<ul id="user-list"></ul>

<template id="user-row">
  <li class="user-row">
    <img class="avatar" width="40" height="40" alt="">
    <span class="name"></span>
    <span class="role"></span>
  </li>
</template>

<script>
  const users = [
    { name: 'Sara Ahmadi', role: 'Designer', avatar: '/a/sara.jpg' },
    { name: 'John Miller', role: 'Backend',  avatar: '/a/john.jpg' }
  ];

  const template = document.getElementById('user-row');
  const list = document.getElementById('user-list');

  for (const user of users) {
    const row = template.content.cloneNode(true);
    row.querySelector('.name').textContent = user.name;
    row.querySelector('.role').textContent = user.role;
    row.querySelector('.avatar').src = user.avatar;
    list.append(row);
  }
</script>
```

**Why this works:** Content inside `<template>` is parsed once at load into an inert `DocumentFragment`: it is not rendered, does not load its images, and cannot match selectors — so you get a pre-validated DOM skeleton at zero runtime cost. `cloneNode(true)` then deep-copies that fragment cheaply. Assigning data via `textContent` (not `innerHTML`) means user-supplied strings are inserted as text nodes and can never execute — string-concatenation templates are a classic XSS vector precisely because they reparse HTML on every render. The markup also stays in one place where designers can edit it, instead of being buried inside JavaScript string literals.

```css
/* Specificity: 0-0-1 (element) */
p { color: gray; }

/* 0-1-0 (class) — beats the element selector */
.note { color: navy; }

/* 0-1-1 (class + element) — beats .note alone */
.note p { color: darkgreen; }

/* 0-2-0 — two classes beat one */
.note.important { color: crimson; }

/* Last tie-breaker: same specificity → source order wins */
.note.important { color: firebrick; }
```

**Why this works:** When multiple declarations target the same element and property, the cascade first compares *origin and importance* (author styles beat user-agent defaults, `!important` flips the order), then *specificity*, then *source order*. Specificity is computed as a three-part count: IDs (1-0-0), classes/attributes/pseudo-classes (0-1-0), and elements/pseudo-elements (0-0-1) — the counts are compared column by column, so a single ID outranks any number of classes. Inline `style` attributes sit above all selector-based rules. In the example, `.note.important` (0-2-0) beats `.note p` (0-1-1) because the class column wins before the element column is even compared. Two rules with identical specificity resolve by whichever appears later — which is why putting overrides at the end of your stylesheet beats adding `!important`, and why `!important` sprinkled everywhere eventually makes a codebase unfixable.

```css
.card {
  /* Inherited by all descendants: */
  color: #1e293b;
  font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
  line-height: 1.6;

  /* NOT inherited — set where you need it: */
  border: 1px solid #cbd5e1;
  padding: 1rem;
  background: white;
}

.card a {
  color: inherit;   /* take parent color instead of UA blue */
}

.dialog {
  all: unset;            /* strip every declaration */
  all: revert;           /* roll back to user-agent/parent value */
}
```

**Why this works:** Text-related properties (`color`, `font-*`, `line-height`, `letter-spacing`, `text-align`, `visibility`) inherit by default because styling a container is expected to style its text. Box-model and layout properties (`width`, `margin`, `padding`, `border`, `background`, `display`, `position`) do not inherit, because flowing them down would give every child the parent's padding — visually nonsensical. The four explicit keywords work on any property: `inherit` forces the parent's computed value, `initial` applies the spec default (e.g. `color` → black, not your theme's color), `unset` means "inherit if the property normally inherits, otherwise initial", and `revert` rolls back to the browser stylesheet. This is why setting `color` on `<body>` styles the whole page, while you must add borders to each element that needs one.

```css
*,
*::before,
*::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

.panel {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 2rem;
  border: 4px solid #2563eb;
  /* total rendered width stays 100% of parent */
}

/* content-box equivalent: rendered width = 100% + 4rem + 8px,
   which overflows its container */
```

**Why this works:** The default `box-sizing: content-box` makes the `width` property describe only the content area; padding and border are then *added on top*, so an element declared `width: 300px; padding: 20px` actually renders 340px wide. `border-box` redefines `width` to describe the final border edge — padding and border eat into the declared width instead of growing it. This matches how humans reason about sizes ("a 300px card is 300px wide") and makes systems like `width: 50%` plus padding compose predictably: two 50% elements plus a gap fit in one row instead of wrapping. The universal selector with `::before`/`::after` covers pseudo-elements too, and the rule is inherited-safe because `box-sizing` is not inherited — hence the explicit `*` reset rather than setting it on `<body>`.

```html
<div class="page">
  <div class="modal-wrapper"><div class="modal">I must be on top</div></div>
  <div class="sticky-header">header</div>
</div>

<style>
  /* Creates a NEW stacking context: z-index of .modal
     is trapped inside it. page has z-index auto, so the
     wrapper participates in page's context with level 1. */
  .page { transform: scale(1); }

  .modal-wrapper { position: absolute; z-index: 1; }
  .modal         { position: absolute; z-index: 99999; }

  /* Fix: give the wrapper a higher level than the header,
     or remove the transform that created the context. */
  .sticky-header { position: sticky; z-index: 2; }
</style>
```

**Why this works:** `z-index` only ranks elements *within the same stacking context* — it never fights across contexts. A new context is created by `position` with a `z-index` other than `auto`, but also by `transform`, `filter`, `opacity` below 1, `will-change`, `isolation: isolate`, `backdrop-filter`, `contain`, and more. Once `.page` has a transform, the modal's 99999 means nothing outside `.page`; the header compares only against the wrapper's effective level. Fixes, in order of preference: (1) remove the accidental context-creating property, (2) raise the wrapper's `z-index` above the competing element, (3) render the overlay at the document root instead of nesting it, or (4) use `isolation: isolate` deliberately to fence a subtree's stacking so its internal z-index values cannot leak out. `z-index: 99999` is a code smell — first find which context you are actually in (DevTools: Layout → stacking order) before raising numbers.

```css
.center-screen {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;  /* main (horizontal) axis */
  align-items: center;      /* cross (vertical) axis */
  min-height: 100dvh;       /* dynamic viewport height */
}

/* Same result in one line: */
.center-grid {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  min-height: 100dvh;
}
```

**Why this works:** Flexbox centers relative to the element's own box, so a container of known height is all you need — no absolute positioning, no `transform` hacks. `justify-content` distributes along the main axis and `align-items` along the cross axis; with the default `flex-direction: row` those are horizontal and vertical respectively. `100dvh` (dynamic viewport height) instead of `100vh` avoids the classic mobile bug where browser chrome makes a `100vh` container taller than the visible screen. The grid version, `place-items: center`, is shorthand for `align-items` + `justify-items` and centers the *item inside its track* — for a single child it is the most concise correct solution. These methods center content of *unknown* size, which old `margin: auto`-only tricks and negative offsets could never guarantee.

```html
<body class="layout">
  <header>Header</header>
  <main>Content — may be short or very long</main>
  <footer>Footer</footer>
</body>

<style>
  .layout {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    min-height: 100dvh;
  }

  main {
    flex: 1 0 auto;   /* grow to absorb free space */
  }
</style>
```

**Why this works:** The container (`body`) is a column flexbox with `min-height: 100dvh`, so it always fills the viewport — but is allowed to grow taller than it when content overflows. With only `flex: 1` on `main`, the free space calculation gives the surplus to main, pushing the footer to the bottom edge on short pages. The `0` in `flex: 1 0 auto` sets `flex-shrink: 0`, which matters on long pages: main refuses to shrink below its content size, so the body simply grows and the footer naturally ends up below the fold — the same ruleset handles both cases with no media queries or JavaScript measuring. Old solutions (absolute-positioned footer + padding-bottom spacer) broke the moment content height changed; flexbox computes it live.

```html
<body class="grail">
  <header>Header</header>
  <nav>Nav</nav>
  <main>Main</main>
  <aside>Ads</aside>
  <footer>Footer</footer>
</body>

<style>
  .grail {
    display: grid;
    grid-template:
      "header header" auto
      "nav    main"   1fr
      "footer footer" auto
      / 200px 1fr;
    min-height: 100dvh;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  header { grid-area: header; }
  nav    { grid-area: nav; }
  main   { grid-area: main; }
  aside  { display: none; }
  footer { grid-area: footer; }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .grail {
      grid-template:
        "header header header" auto
        "nav    main   aside"  1fr
        "footer footer footer" auto
        / 200px 1fr 250px;
    }
    aside { display: block; }
  }
</style>
```

**Why this works:** `grid-template` names areas once and defines both the row track sizes and column track sizes in the same declaration, so the whole layout is a single readable map — each row's quoted strings are the column layout, the slash section lists the column widths. `1fr` on the middle row makes the content area absorb all leftover height, which is what keeps the footer pinned at the bottom. The mobile breakpoint doesn't reposition anything in markup: `aside` simply drops out of the named areas, so grid auto-places it nowhere (hidden with `display: none`), and nav stacks above main because the middle row becomes one column. Unlike the historical float-based holy grail (which needed equal-height hacks and fragile negative margins), the grid version guarantees equal-height columns because items in the same row stretch to the track height by default.

```css
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
  gap: 24px;
  max-width: 1200px;
  margin-inline: auto;
}

.col-6  { grid-column: span 6; }
.col-4  { grid-column: span 4; }
.col-8  { grid-column: span 8; }
.col-12 { grid-column: span 12; }

@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .sm-col-4 { grid-column: span 4; }
}

/* Usage: two half-width cards + one full-width */
/* <div class="grid">
     <article class="col-12 col-6"></article>
     <article class="col-12 col-6"></article>
     <article class="col-12"></article>
   </div> */
```

**Why this works:** `repeat(12, 1fr)` creates twelve equal flexible tracks; `1fr` means each track gets an equal share of the space *left after* the gutters are removed, because `gap` reserves real track space — no more negative-margin + inner-padding gutter math from the float/Bootstrap 3 era. `grid-column: span N` makes an item stretch across N tracks; because the default flow is row-auto-placement, items automatically wrap to the next row when the remaining span doesn't fit. The mobile-first pattern works by listing the small-screen span first and overriding inside the media query — same specificity, later in source, so the cascade does the work without `!important`. Items remain stretch-aligned to their tracks by default, which is why cards in a row stay equal-height for free.

```css
.card-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns:
    repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(260px, 100%), 1fr));
  gap: 1.5rem;
}
```

**Why this works:** This is the auto-responsive pattern: `minmax(260px, 1fr)` says every column must be *at least* 260px but may grow to share the leftover space equally. `auto-fit` asks the browser to fit as many such columns as possible into the container and collapse any empty ones. On a 1000px container you get 3 columns (1000/260 → 3 fit, each ≈317px); at 550px you get 2; at 300px, 1 — columns appear and disappear as the viewport changes, with zero breakpoints. The `min(260px, 100%)` guard handles the degenerate narrow case: on a 200px-wide container, a bare 260px minimum would force horizontal overflow, but `min(260px, 100%)` caps the minimum at the container width so the single column shrinks instead. Prefer `auto-fit` over `auto-fill` when empty tracks should collapse (cards stretch to fill), and `auto-fill` when you want placeholder tracks reserved so a lone item doesn't blow up to full width.

```html
<div class="dash">
  <div class="d-side">Sidebar</div>
  <div class="d-main">Main chart</div>
  <div class="d-stats">Stats</div>
</div>

<style>
  .dash {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .dash {
      grid-template-areas:
        "stats"
        "main"
        "side";
      grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto;
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 701px) {
    .dash {
      grid-template-areas:
        "side main"
        "side stats";
      grid-template-columns: 240px 1fr;
      grid-template-rows: 1fr auto;
    }
  }

  .d-side  { grid-area: side; }
  .d-main  { grid-area: main; }
  .d-stats { grid-area: stats; }
</style>
```

**Why this works:** `grid-template-areas` decouples visual order from DOM order: each element is assigned a name, and the template is just an ASCII map of where names go. Rearranging the dashboard is editing a text diagram — swapping `"stats"` above `"main"` moves the whole element, no `order` hacks or absolute positioning. The layout stays valid because every row's quoted strings must have the same number of columns and each name must form one rectangle; the browser enforces this and rejects invalid templates. Keeping the element↔name assignments outside the media queries means each breakpoint only redefines the map. One caution: because visual order now differs from DOM order, maintain a sensible source order (sidebar last on mobile here) so keyboard/screen-reader navigation follows a logical sequence.

```html
<article class="product-card">
  <img src="shoe.jpg" alt="Running shoe" width="400" height="300">
  <div class="card-body">
    <h3>Aero Runner</h3>
    <p>Lightweight daily trainer.</p>
    <button>Add to cart</button>
  </div>
</article>

<style>
  .card-wrap { container-type: inline-size; }

  .product-card { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }

  @container (min-width: 400px) {
    .product-card {
      flex-direction: row;
      gap: 1rem;
    }
    .product-card img {
      width: 40%;
      height: auto;
      object-fit: cover;
    }
  }
</style>
```

**Why this works:** Media queries only know the *viewport* width, but the same card gets rendered in a 320px sidebar and an 800px main column — the viewport tells you nothing about the card's actual space. `container-type: inline-size` registers an ancestor as a query container, and `@container` conditions then test *that ancestor's* inline size, so the card switches to its horizontal variant exactly when its own box is wide enough, wherever it is placed. This makes components genuinely portable: drop the same markup into a grid cell, a modal, or a drawer and it self-adapts. `inline-size` containment also has a side effect the layout must respect: the container's inline size can no longer depend on its contents (contents can't size the container, preventing layout loops). Type the container with a `container-name` when several nested containers exist so `@container card (min-width: 400px)` targets the right one.

```css
:root {
  --bg: #ffffff;
  --fg: #0f172a;
  --accent: #2563eb;
  color-scheme: light dark;
}

@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root:not([data-theme="light"]) {
    --bg: #0b1220;
    --fg: #e2e8f0;
    --accent: #60a5fa;
  }
}

:root[data-theme="dark"] {
  --bg: #0b1220;
  --fg: #e2e8f0;
  --accent: #60a5fa;
}

body {
  background: var(--bg);
  color: var(--fg);
  transition: background-color 0.3s, color 0.3s;
}
```

**Why this works:** Custom properties are inherited live values, so redefining `--bg`/`--fg` on `:root` recolors every descendant that reads them with `var()` — no selector duplication, one source of truth per theme. The OS preference is respected by default via the media query; the `:not([data-theme="light"])` guard lets a manual toggle *override* the OS setting instead of fighting it, and `data-theme="dark"` forces dark even on light-OS machines. `color-scheme: light dark` additionally tells the browser which palettes the page supports, fixing form controls, scrollbars and the canvas background in dark mode. Persist the choice in `localStorage` and set `data-theme` before first paint (inline script in `<head>`) to avoid a theme flash; three-state toggles (light/dark/auto) should just remove the attribute to fall back to the media query.

```css
:root {
  /* clamp(min, preferred, max) — fluid type in one line */
  --h1-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);

  /* min() picks the smaller: caps a width but allows shrink */
  --content-width: min(72ch, 100% - 2rem);

  /* max() picks the larger: guarantees a floor */
  --tap-target: max(44px, 3rem);
}

.sidebar {
  /* calc mixes units that plain CSS cannot reconcile */
  width: calc(100% - var(--nav-width, 240px) - 2 * 1.5rem);
}

.hero {
  margin-top: clamp(1rem, 4vh + 1rem, 4rem);
}
```

**Why this works:** `calc()` evaluates an expression mixing units (`%` minus `px`), which is impossible in a plain value — it resolves at computed-value time against the actual layout. `min()`/`max()` are just comparisons that resolve the same way; the crucial mental model is that they behave *opposite* to their names for sizing: `min(72ch, 100% - 2rem)` caps a maximum width (the element can still be smaller), while `max(44px, 3rem)` sets a minimum. `clamp(MIN, VAL, MAX)` is exactly `max(MIN, min(VAL, MAX))` and is the workhorse for fluid values: the example heading scales with the viewport between hard floors and ceilings, replacing a whole ladder of media-query font sizes. Inside `clamp` the middle term can itself be an expression (`4vh + 1rem`) — that offset keeps text from getting too small relative to line height at the extremes. All three functions accept `var()` references, which is how design-token systems stay responsive.

```css
:root {
  --brand: #2563eb;
  --surface: #ffffff;
}

.btn {
  background: var(--brand);
  color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand), black 55%);
}

.btn:hover {
  background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--brand), black 15%);
}

.badge {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand) 12%, transparent);
  color: var(--brand);
}

.alert {
  background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--brand), red 40%);
}
```

**Why this works:** `color-mix(in <colorspace>, A p%, B q%)` blends two colors at computed time — the percentages must total 100% (the remainder defaults to the other color). Because it references `var(--brand)`, recoloring a theme with one variable instantly updates every derived tint, shade, and alpha variant; no parallel palette of hand-picked hex codes to keep in sync. The interpolation space matters: `srgb` mixes in gamma space and is the fast, familiar choice, while perceptual spaces like `oklab`/`oklch` produce midpoints that look uniformly blended to the eye (mixing blue→white in srgb drifts purple; in oklab it goes through a cleaner light blue). Mixing with `transparent` is the modern replacement for `rgb(... / 0.12)` alpha math and works even when the base color comes from a variable you don't control. Typical production recipe: hover = mix 12–15% black, borders = 30%, subtle backgrounds = 10–15% with `transparent`.

```css
.alert {
  /* Physical (direction-blind): */
  margin-left: auto;
  padding-left: 1rem;
  border-left: 4px solid currentColor;
  text-align: left;

  /* Logical (adapts to writing mode): */
  margin-inline-start: auto;
  padding-inline-start: 1rem;
  border-inline-start: 4px solid currentColor;
  text-align: start;
}

/* Axis shorthand pair: */
.section {
  padding-block: 2rem;   /* top + bottom */
  margin-inline: auto;   /* left + right */
}
```

**Why this works:** Physical properties (`left`, `right`, `top`, `bottom`) are pinned to the screen's geometry, but in right-to-left languages like Persian and Arabic "left" and "right" swap meaning — a card built with `padding-left` looks correct in English and broken in RTL. Logical properties map to *flow-relative* axes: `inline` is the axis text flows along, `block` is the axis lines stack along, and `start`/`end` flip automatically with `direction` or `writing-mode`. The exact same stylesheet therefore mirrors itself for free under `dir="rtl"`, with no second RTL stylesheet and no `[dir="rtl"]` override blocks. `inset-inline-start` likewise replaces `left` for positioned elements. The `*-block`/`*-inline` shorthands also set both sides in one declaration. New projects should standardize on logical properties; the physical ones remain only for genuinely screen-anchored cases like `position: fixed` overlays relative to the viewport.

```css
/* Old way: repeat the list for every combinator */
/* header a:hover, main a:hover, footer a:hover { ... } */

/* :is() — specificity = most specific argument (0-0-1) */
:is(header, main, footer) a:hover {
  color: var(--accent);
}

/* :where() — specificity = ZERO, always easy to override */
:where(h1, h2, h3) {
  margin-block: 0.5em;
}

/* :has() — the "parent selector": style the card that
   CONTAINS an image differently */
.card:has(img) {
  grid-template-columns: 160px 1fr;
}

.card:has(img) .card-title {
  font-size: 1.1rem;
}

/* Form feedback without JS classes: */
label:has(input:invalid)::after {
  content: " ⚠ required";
}
```

**Why this works:** `:is()` and `:where()` take a *forgiving* selector list (one invalid entry is ignored instead of killing the whole rule, unlike a hand-written list) and hoist a shared combinator, so `:is(header, main, footer) a:hover` replaces three full selectors. The difference is specificity: `:is()` counts its most specific argument, while `:where()` always contributes zero — that makes `:where()` the right tool for base/defaults you want any later rule (even a bare `h2`) to override. `:has()` finally lets a selector look *upward*: `.card:has(img)` styles the card based on its contents, which previously required JavaScript adding classes. `:has()` has the specificity of its argument and accepts relative selectors (`:has(> img)`, `:has(+ p)`), enabling sibling logic too. Because it can restyle large subtrees on DOM mutation, keep `:has()` selectors shallow to avoid layout thrash.

```css
/* Decorative quote mark, no extra HTML element */
blockquote::before {
  content: "\201C";
  font-size: 3rem;
  line-height: 1;
  color: #94a3b8;
}

/* Required-field marker driven by data attribute */
[data-required]::after {
  content: " *";
  color: #dc2626;
}

/* Underline reveal on hover */
.nav-link {
  position: relative;
}
.nav-link::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  bottom: -4px;
  width: 100%;
  height: 2px;
  background: currentColor;
  transform: scaleX(0);
  transform-origin: left;
  transition: transform 0.25s;
}
.nav-link:hover::after {
  transform: scaleX(1);
}
```

**Why this works:** `::before` and `::after` generate a real (styleable) child box inside the element, rendered as its first/last child — but only if `content` is set (even the empty string `""`); without `content` nothing renders, which is the #1 gotcha. Because they are children, they inherit `currentColor`, fonts, and can be positioned relative to a `position: relative` parent — that's what makes the self-contained hover underline possible with zero extra markup. `"\201C"` is a Unicode escape for the typographic left double quote. Generated content is presentation-only: it is not read by all screen readers, is not selectable in some browsers, and cannot contain HTML — use it for decoration (icons, markers, overlays), never for meaningful content that must be accessible.

```css
/* TRANSITION: state A → B, user-triggered */
.button {
  background: var(--accent);
  transition: background-color 0.25s ease-out, transform 0.25s ease-out;
}
.button:hover {
  background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent), black 15%);
  transform: translateY(-2px);
}

/* ANIMATION: multi-step, looping, runs on its own */
.pulse {
  animation: pulse 2s ease-in-out infinite;
}

@keyframes pulse {
  0%   { box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 60%, transparent); }
  70%  { box-shadow: 0 0 0 12px transparent; }
  100% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 transparent; }
}
```

**Why this works:** A `transition` is a passive rule: it interpolates between two computed states when a property *changes* (via `:hover`, a class swap, or JS), and it is automatically reversible — moving the mouse out replays it backwards. Choose transitions for interactive feedback on known endpoints. An `@keyframes` `animation` is proactive: it runs when applied, supports any number of keyframes at arbitrary percentages, loops (`infinite`), can `alternate` direction, and has its own fill/delay/state properties (`animation-play-state`, `animation-fill-mode: forwards`) — choose animations for load sequences, loaders, and anything with more than two states or no user trigger. Performance rule for both: animate only `transform` and `opacity` (compositor-only, no layout/paint) and never animate layout properties like `width`, `top`, or `margin`; `box-shadow` in the keyframe above is acceptable for a rare looping effect but a large-scale `transform` ring is cheaper if it appears in hot paths.

```css
.card {
  transition: transform 0.3s ease;
}

.card:hover {
  transform: translateY(-6px) scale(1.02);
}

.badge {
  /* center without knowing the element's size */
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  translate: -50% -50%;
}
```

**Why this works:** `transform` applies an affine transformation (translate, rotate, scale, skew) to the element's rendered box *after* layout is computed — the element still occupies its original space, so transforms never trigger reflow of surrounding content. Because the transformed result is a compositor-layer operation (when transitioned on its own), the browser can hand it to the GPU and animate at 60fps even during heavy page paint. Moving with `top`/`left` instead recalculates layout every frame, and scaling with `width`/`height` reflows the whole document — both are the classic causes of janky hover effects. Transforms compose right-to-left in the listed order, so `translateY(-6px) scale(1.02)` first scales, then shifts. The independent `translate` property (note: no `transform()` wrapper) combined with `top/left: 50%` is the size-agnostic centering trick: `50%` of the parent minus `50%` of the element's own box.

```css
/* 1. Legibility scrim over a hero image */
.hero {
  background-image:
    linear-gradient(to top, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.7) 0%, transparent 55%),
    url('hero.jpg');
  background-size: cover;
}

/* 2. Fade-out text edge when content overflows */
.fade-clamp {
  mask-image: linear-gradient(to right, black 85%, transparent);
}

/* 3. Conic-gradient progress ring */
.ring {
  width: 96px;
  aspect-ratio: 1;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background:
    conic-gradient(var(--accent) 0 270deg, #e2e8f0 270deg 360deg);
  mask: radial-gradient(farthest-side, transparent 62%, black 63%);
}
```

**Why this works:** Gradients are generated images, so they stack in `background-image` layers like image URLs — the first listed layer paints on top, which is why the dark scrim sits above the photo and the `to top` direction keeps the dark side at the bottom. Using `rgb(0 0 0 / 0.7)` instead of a solid color makes the overlay blend with whatever is beneath. The fade-out edge uses a gradient as a `mask-image` (alpha mask): black = fully visible, transparent = hidden, so text dissolves exactly like native scroll-end fades without a PNG. The progress ring is two tricks combined: `conic-gradient` sweeps accent color over 270 of 360 degrees (75% progress), and the radial-gradient `mask` punches a hole in the middle, turning the disc into a ring — one element, no SVG, and the percentage is just an angle you can set from a custom property via `style="--p: 270deg"` if you extend the pattern.

```html
<div class="thumb-card">
  <img src="photo.jpg" alt="Mountain lake at sunrise" width="1200" height="800">
  <h3>Trip report</h3>
</div>

<style>
  .thumb-card img {
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center;
    border-radius: 8px;
  }
</style>
```

**Why this works:** `aspect-ratio: 16 / 9` tells the browser the preferred width-to-height ratio of the box, so with `width: 100%` the height is *derived* — every card in a grid gets an identical image slot with zero JavaScript measuring and, critically, zero layout shift before the image loads (the box exists at the right size immediately). `object-fit: cover` then controls how the actual bitmap fills that box: `cover` scales the image up until it covers the whole box and crops the overflow, preserving the photo's own proportions (no stretching), while the default `fill` would distort it and `contain` would letterbox it. `object-position: center` picks which part survives the crop (e.g. `top` for portraits with heads). The HTML `width`/`height` attributes provide the browser's pre-CSS fallback ratio so even before the stylesheet lands, space is reserved — together the two mechanisms eliminate cumulative layout shift on media-heavy pages.

```css
/* 1. Global kill-switch: neutralize decorative motion */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *,
  *::before,
  *::after {
    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    animation-iteration-count: 1 !important;
    transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    scroll-behavior: auto !important;
  }
}

/* 2. Better: enhance deliberately */
.fade-in {
  opacity: 1;
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  .fade-in {
    opacity: 0;
    animation: rise 0.6s ease-out forwards;
  }
  @keyframes rise {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(12px); }
    to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  }
}

/* 3. Pair with JS + CSS var for parallax */
:root { --parallax: 1; }
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  :root { --parallax: 0; }
}
/* JS: layer.style.transform = `translateY(${y * getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue('--parallax')}px)` */
```

**Why this works:** The reduce media query matches users who asked their OS for less motion (vestibular disorders, motion sickness) — for them, flashing slideshows and parallax are not an aesthetic issue but a health issue. The blanket `0.01ms` pattern is the safe default because it ends animations *immediately* while still firing `animationend` events (fully zeroing duration can break JS that waits for those events) and stops infinite loops with `iteration-count: 1`. The progressive-enhancement variant (option 2) is architecturally cleaner: motion exists only inside `no-preference`, so reduced-motion users get the finished state by default with no `!important` arms race. For JS-driven effects, mirror the preference with `matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)')` or a CSS custom property the script reads, so CSS and JS never disagree. Note this replaces *decorative* motion only — functional feedback (e.g. a progress bar) should still exist, just without flourish.

```css
:root {
  /* One fluid step reused by the whole scale */
  --fluid-step: clamp(0.25rem, 0.5vw + 0.125rem, 1rem);

  --fs-sm:  clamp(0.875rem, 0.8rem + 0.35vw, 1rem);
  --fs-base: clamp(1rem, 0.9rem + 0.5vw, 1.25rem);
  --fs-xl:  clamp(1.75rem, 1.4rem + 2vw, 3rem);

  /* Spacing never exceeds a max, but floors at a minimum */
  --space-section: min(8vh, var(--fluid-step) * 8);
}

body { font-size: var(--fs-base); line-height: 1.6; }

h1 { font-size: var(--fs-xl); }

section + section {
  margin-block-start: var(--space-section);
}
```

**Why this works:** Each `clamp()` interpolates between a mobile floor and a desktop ceiling with a viewport-driven middle term; because the interpolation is continuous, headings never snap between media-query sizes — they *grow* with the viewport, which reads as intentional typography. Writing every size as `small + coefficient * vw` keeps the scale coherent: the coefficients differ per step, so `h1` grows faster than body text and the hierarchy widens on large screens (a real type scale) instead of everything scaling uniformly. The `--fluid-step` variable composes into larger rhythm values, keeping vertical rhythm proportional to the type scale. `min(8vh, ...)` picks the smaller of a viewport-relative cap and the step-based value — the general rule: `min()` for maximum-like constraints (it can still shrink), `max()` for floors, `clamp()` for both bounds at once. Add a `@media (prefers-reduced-transparency)`-style check only where motion is involved; pure sizing like this is safe everywhere, including print styles.
